there's nothing like a good kung fu  movie is there but which films are  champions of the genre here's our list  of the best kung fu movies of all time  does one of your favorites make the cut  commonly praised as one of the best kung  fu movies of the 1990s iron monkey was  proof that even though its golden period  had ended the kung fu genre still had  some life in it based in part on chinese  folk hero wang fehung and his father's  encounter with the mysterious martial  artist known only as the iron monkey  it's a film that boasts some first-rate  action scenes full of lightning-fast  kicks and epic slow motion action donnie  yen stars as wang ka ying a chinese  physician and skilled martial artist  forced to battle the masked robin  hood-like vigilante called the iron  monkey after his son is kidnapped by a  corrupt city official when the iron  monkey proves his heroism by saving wang  kaying's son the two fighters set out to  combat the city's bureaucratic  politicians together originally released  in 1993 iron monkey was warmly received  by critics and moviegoers earning an  approval rating of 91 percent on rotten  tomatoes it was even listed number 99 on  empire magazine's the 100 best films of  world cinema list iron monkey would  later see an expanded release in the us  after quentin tarantino convinced  miramax to acquire the distribution  rights the beloved director kung fu  super fans stamp of approval broadened  its appeal even further  the tone style is immensely strong  and immune to nearly any weapon  when it's properly used it's almost  invincible  a kung fu film with the loyal cult  following five deadly venoms is a  classic product of the shaw brothers  studio the seeds of which launched in  1925 and would go on to become hong  kong's largest production company yang  taya is a young martial artist  instructed by his dying master to seek  out the other former students all of  whom have become powerful masters of a  specific fighting style and to eliminate  any who are using their abilities for  nefarious purposes these five students  fighting styles have each earned a  specific name the centipede the snake  the scorpion the lizard and the toad  they are all based on the five poisonous  creatures from chinese folklore and yang  tia was taught a little bit of each  combat style by today's standards five  deadly venoms may be seen as somewhat  cheesy and dated but it is exactly this  over stylization that makes it an  enjoyable essential kung fu romp and  mandatory viewing for any would-be fan  of the genre one of the most influential  kung fu movies ever made it's been  referenced in everything from kill bill  to tupac's five deadly venoms  additionally the movie's dialogue  appears in several wu-tang clan songs  like the mystery of chess boxing and  intro shaolin finger jab  [Applause]  after bruce lee's death in 1973 the kung  fu film entered a strange state of limbo  at its peak of popularity but suddenly  without its most prominent leading man  genre producers desperately scrambled  for potential stars to succeed lee few  actors matched his intensity gravitas  and on-screen presence instead they  turned to an actor who had the  physicality but wanted to take the genre  in a wholly unexpected direction that  actor was jackie chan when speaking to  the new york times in 1995 about his  early years in the business chan said i  wanted to be like a chaplain or buster  keaton but all the martial arts  directors i worked with wanted me to  copy bruce lee after making a name for  himself with the 1978 hong kong kung fu  comedy drunken master chan tried and  failed to break into hollywood he left  the american film industry dissatisfied  and disappointed in the quality of films  like battle creek brawl resolving to  make his own movie chan went to work  co-writing directing and starring in  police story the first in a series of  successful action crime movies in police  story chan plays a hong kong police  detective framed for the murder of  another officer by a powerful drug  kingpin blending slapstick comedy with  large-scale action sequences police  story was a massive blockbuster in  eastern markets when first released the  movie went on to win the 1986 hong kong  film award for best film matt zoller  sites of roger ebert.com praised the  movie's famous mall fight scene calling  it a masterpiece of choreography and one  of the greatest stunts in the history of  movies in 2016 it was ranked the fourth  best action film in a timeout poll which  consisted of critics filmmakers and  stunt actors  master of the flying guillotine is a  kung fu film within the usha genre a  martial arts movie blending elements of  fantasy and magical realism  traditionally set in ancient china and  while it may be lesser known than most  other kung fu films that came out around  the same time it remains an influential  and enjoyable movie a sequel to director  co-writer and star jimmy yang woo's  earlier one-armed boxer master of the  flying guillotine sees the previous  film's titular protagonist defending  himself from a vengeful blind assassin  wielding a deadly buzzsaw hat that  decapitates anyone who wears it named by  quentin tarantino is one of his favorite  films of all time it's easy to see his  attraction to the flick it's pure 70s  camp full of cartoonish violence and  flashy gloriously ridiculous fighting  moves as an example in one of the film's  most famous scenes jimmy wang's boxer  manages to punch the assassin through  the roof and into his own coffin since  its release master of the flying  guillotine has been praised by  publications like the new york times as  one of the best kung fu films it has  even been referred to as the quote holy  grail of hong kong martial arts movies  of the 70s by los angeles times kevin  thomas  in the 1990s the kung fu genre saw a  resurgence in popularity this was thanks  in large part to films starring donnie  yen and jet li who were among the most  charismatic and proficient martial  artists turned actors to come along  since bruce lee and jackie chan in the  film that rocketed lee to stardom the  actor would appear as the legendary folk  hero wang hung a physician as talented  in the medical field as he is in kung fu  lee's wong fehung trains a local militia  to combat a criminal martial arts  syndicate as well as encroaching western  colonizers praised by critics once upon  a time in china retains an 88 approval  rating on rotten tomatoes and was  nominated for best film at the 11th hong  kong film awards the movie is mostly  remembered today for its incredible  action scenes and for league using  everything from an umbrella to bamboo  sticks as lethal weapons but its story  about retaining one's cultural roots in  the face of rapid westernization makes  for a surprisingly political movie  hidden under the guise of a kung fu  flick  [Music]  another undisputed classic from shaw  brothers studio the 36th chamber of  shaolin is regarded by many fans of the  genre as one of the best kung fu films  ever made it jump-started the careers of  both its director lao karlion and its  star gordon liu in the eastern film  industry  lou plays a young student named lou yud  whose entire school is systematically  killed off by a brutal government regime  seeking vengeance the student travels to  a shaolin temple in order to learn kung  fu he is put to the test when he must  pass through the temple's 36 chambers  each emphasizing key combat and fighting  techniques to sharpen one's mind and  body a rare film where the training  scenes are as meticulously orchestrated  and fun to watch as the actual fight  sequences the 36th chamber of shaolin  was a milestone in the kung fu genre it  was proof that memorable kung fu films  could still emerge despite the drop-off  in popularity that post-bruce leaflix  suffered in the late 70s praised by  critics it has an audience approval  rating of 90 on rotten tomatoes and was  listed at number 22 in timeout's poll of  the 101 best action movies it was also a  direct influence on the wu-tang clan in  addition to referencing the film in the  name of their debut album enter the wu  tang 36 chambers wu-tang clan member  mast akilah took his name from one of  the film's alternate titles the master  killer  sequels rarely measure up to the success  and popularity of their predecessors  however the legend of drunken master not  only manages to match the strengths of  its original movie it surpasses it  entirely also known as legend master 2  the legend of drunken master is a major  improvement on the original drunken  master in every conceivable way the  sequel features better stunts better  action and better comedy released 16  years after the original drunken master  chan once again stars as folk hero wang  fehung a master of drinking and martial  arts both of which he combines to form  his unique drunken boxing fighting style  pressured by his father to give up  drinking and fighting for good wang  fehung must decide between obeying his  father's wishes and stopping a foreign  plot to steal priceless artifacts from  his homeland the legend of drunken  master earned near universal acclaim  including a number of prestigious awards  following its release  in 2015 the british film institute  included it on their 10 best action  movies list when discussing the final  showdown in his review of the film for  the chicago sun times roger ebert said  it may not be possible to film a better  fight scene  [Music]  kung fu films seldom win or earn  nominations from international film  festivals in 2000 however an usha film  broke through the mode delivering an  action-packed narrative with a  surprising amount of tender emotion  hidden beneath its violent surface  in crouching tiger hidden dragon hong  kong action star chao young fat plays  veteran warrior lee mubai who entrusts  his faithful 400 year old sword known as  the green destiny to his friend after he  decides to retire when a nimble young  thief steals the sword for herself  mubai's friend embarks on a journey to  recover it facing her own complex  feelings toward her old friend along the  way blending romance with kung fu  doesn't always make for a great combo  but in director anglie's very capable  hands a tender portrait of forbidden  love is delivered between rapid-fire  kicks and captivating sword fights  universally acclaimed upon release  crouching tiger hidden dragon won more  than 40 awards the film earned an oscar  nomination for best picture and won best  foreign language film best art direction  best original score and best  cinematography it's also far and away  the highest grossing foreign language  film ever released in the u.s  kung fu hustle is a modern kung fu film  more along the lines of a spoof than an  homage to the martial arts genre it was  director writer and star stephen chao's  follow-up to his hilarious hit shaolin  soccer a film that satirized the more  recent stunts found in martial arts  flicks of the early 2000s kung fu hustle  was designed as a parody of classic kung  fu movies featuring several retired  genre actors in prominent roles in the  film chao stars as sing an aspiring  low-level criminal trying to make a name  for himself as a legitimate bad guy by  joining the infamous axe gang when the  axe gang begins to wreak havoc on a  local slum called pig sty alley only to  find it populated by several kung fu  masters who have gone into hiding sing  must decide whether to join the gang or  save the neighborhood from certain  destruction while the fight scenes are  incredibly well done it's the movie's  comedic style that makes kung fu hustle  so enjoyable abandoning the laws of  physics completely characters are able  to move with cartoonish speed and  agility leading to a glowing review by  roger ebert in which he refers to it as  jackie chan and buster keaton meet  quentin tarantino and bugs bunny  praised by everyone from james gunn to  bill murray it may not have the most  hard-hitting action but it's definitely  the funniest kung fu movie you're likely  to find anywhere  the kung fu genre may have reached its  peak with bruce lee but more recent  movies have kept the genre alive for a  new generation to enjoy foremost among  these is eatman a fictionalized biopic  about the grand master of the martial  art wing chun who is known for training  some of the world's greatest martial  artists including bruce lee the first in  a five film series eep man stars donnie  yen as the legendary martial artist who  faces constant challenges from local  combat experts and professional soldiers  trying to prove themselves during the  sino-japanese war while the historical  accuracy of the film is debatable the  success it achieved is not earning a  positive reception from critics the  movie would go on to launch a popular  film franchise ignite yen's career and  earn heaps of awards in variety  magazine's review of flick derek ellie  wrote  jen who's taking on real star charisma  in middle age is aces eep with the  simple dignity that exactly mirrors the  movie zone and a gracefulness in combat  that's very different from his trademark  whiplash style on rotten tomatoes the  movie has an 86 approval rating with the  critical consensus stating at once  beholden to the established conventions  of the genre and delightfully subversive  of them eep man is one of the most  exciting and refreshingly character  driven martial arts films in years  a man who adores all things film when  quentin tarantino set out to create his  own martial arts movie in the early  2000s he blended all of his favorite  b-movie genres together into the massive  kung fu extravaganza that is kill bill  spanning two films and clocking in at a  total run time of four hours kill bill  stars uma thurman as a woman initially  known only as the bride she was left for  dead by her former top-notch mercenary  team led by her ex-lover bill waking  from the coma he and his gang put her in  thurman's bride embarks on a  globe-trotting mission to hunt down all  of her former teammates who wronged her  eventually coming face to face with bill  himself one of tarantino's classics the  ingenuity of kill bill lies in its  endless references to kung fu films near  and dear to his heart a lovingly made  homage to a movie genre he clearly holds  dear it was the subject of widespread  critical praise it earned several bafta  and golden globe nominations including  best actress and best supporting actor  [Music]  in many ways what clint eastwood or john  wayne was for the western bruce lee was  for martial arts movies but while  eastwood and wayne had decades and  dozens of films to establish their  legacies lee did it in a fraction of  that time enter the dragon shows lee as  a skilled martial artist sent undercover  to compete in a fighting tournament  organized by a dangerous international  criminal almost inarguably the most  influential kung fu movie of all time  enter the dragon is considered lee's  masterpiece it's also one of the most  financially successful films ever made  grossing over 400 times its estimated  budget of 850 000  at the time of its release enter the  dragon earned mixed to average reviews  though later critics would be far more  generous complimenting the movie's  action music and lee's performance the  new york times howard thompson wrote of  the film the picture is expertly made  and well meshed it moves like lightning  and brims with color it is also the most  savagely murderous and numbing hand  hacker not a gun in it you will ever see  anywhere the enduring influence of enter  the dragon can be found not only in  nearly every kung fu film that has  followed since but in modern video game  series manga and anime as well the plot  of the film resembles the basic premise  of mortal kombat and the movie has been  cited as a key influence on the dragon  ball franchise  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite stuff are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]

